%U A399382 1,1,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,1,1,3,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,2,2,2,1
%N A399382 a(n) is the minimum positive integer k such that k*n+1 is squarefree.
%C A399382 a(n) >= A007424(n+1). In fact, a(n) = A007424(n+1) = 1 if n+1 is squarefree.
%C A399382 It appears that a(n-1) = A399381(n+1) for many values of n > 1. a(n-1) <= A399381(n+1) until a(24) = 3 > 2 = A399381(26).
%C A399382 a(n) exists for all n. For all k > 0, there is some n such that a(n) = k. - _Charles R Greathouse IV_, Aug 28 2026
%C A399382 From _Aidan Markey_, Sep 13 2026: (Start)
%C A399382 If positive integers are selected at random, then the expected number of selections made before reaching a squarefree number is Pi^2/6 = 1.644934... (A013661). However, the average value of the first n terms of this sequence appears to converge to a constant less than Pi^2/6 as n grows arbitrarily large. For example, the average of the first 10^7 terms of this sequence is 1.4694780. This suggests that finding the smallest squarefree number of the form n*k+1 for a specific value of n (A076986) is, on average, more efficient than a random process.
%C A399382 I conjecture that the averages of the first n terms of this sequence and the first n terms of A399381 tend to the same constant as n grows arbitrarily large. (End)
%H A399382 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A399382/b399382.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399382 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A399382/a399382.txt">Java program</a>
%e A399382 a(7) = 2 because 1*7+1 = 8 is not squarefree, but 2*7+1 = 15 is squarefree.
%t A399382 A399382[n_] := NestWhile[# + 1 &, 1, !SquareFreeQ[#*n + 1] &];
%t A399382 Array[A399382, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 07 2026 *)
%o A399382 (Java) // See Links
%o A399382 (PARI) a(n) = my(k=1); while (!issquarefree(k*n+1), k++); k; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 28 2026
%o A399382 (Python)
%o A399382 from itertools import count
%o A399382 from sympy import factorint
%o A399382 def a399382(n): return next(k for k in count(1) if max(factorint(k*n+1).values()) < 2)
%o A399382 print([a399382(n) for n in range(1, 101)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Sep 07 2026
%Y A399382 Cf. A076986 (the resulting squarefrees a(n)*n+1).
%Y A399382 Cf. A005117 (squarefree numbers).
%Y A399382 Similar to A399381, A007424.
%Y A399382 Cf. A399640 (index of the first occurrence of n in this sequence).
